WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.054 --> 00:00:10.144
FAYE MOSS: Hello and welcome back to Superstars in History , the only talk show that features interviews with history's biggest

2
00:00:10.145 --> 00:00:12.798
stars. I'm your host, Faye

3
00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:24.555
Moss. Today's guest was an Olympic gold medalist and three-time World Heavyweight Champion who entertained his fans both in and out of the boxing

4
00:00:24.556 --> 00:00:48.698
ring. Please help me welcome one of the world's greatest athletes, Muhammad

5
00:00:48.699 --> 00:00:56.022
Ali! MUHAMMAD ALI: Faye, I think you've got that wrong. I'm not "one of the world's greatest athletes"—I am the

6
00:00:56.023 --> 00:01:02.876
greatest! FAYE MOSS: Well, I stand corrected! So can you tell us about your

7
00:01:02.877 --> 00:01:08.453
childhood? How did you become a boxer—I mean the greatest

8
00:01:08.454 --> 00:01:16.528
boxer? MUHAMMAD ALI: I was born in Louisville, Kentucky in 1942. My parents gave me a brand new, beautiful red bike for my twelfth

9
00:01:16.529 --> 00:01:22.601
birthday. I rode it downtown to see a fair, and when I came out of the building, my bike had been

10
00:01:22.602 --> 00:01:28.790
stolen. I was so mad I was crying! A policeman named Joe Martin saw me and asked what was

11
00:01:28.791 --> 00:01:32.625
wrong. I told him I wanted to beat up the person who stole my

12
00:01:32.626 --> 00:01:36.682
bike. He said if I wanted to beat somebody up, I needed to know how to

13
00:01:36.683 --> 00:01:40.777
fight. He was a boxing trainer and told me to come down to the gym he

14
00:01:40.778 --> 00:01:43.516
ran. FAYE MOSS: What a

15
00:01:43.517 --> 00:01:49.427
coincidence! MUHAMMAD ALI: It was. Joe was a white man, and at the time blacks and whites couldn't train

16
00:01:49.428 --> 00:01:55.703
together. But Joe's gym was integrated. Joe took me on, became my coach, and taught me how to

17
00:01:55.704 --> 00:01:58.860
fight. I won my first match a few weeks

18
00:01:58.861 --> 00:02:01.917
later. FAYE MOSS: You must have really loved

19
00:02:01.918 --> 00:02:05.920
it. MUHAMMAD ALI: No, I hated every minute of training, but I didn't quit.

20
00:02:05.921 --> 00:02:10.423
I knew if I suffered, I would live the rest of my life as a champion.

21
00:02:10.424 --> 00:02:14.102
Even then I knew I was great—and I wasn't afraid to say so.

22
00:02:14.103 --> 00:02:18.935
The newspapers loved that stuff, and it got me lots of attention.

23
00:02:18.936 --> 00:02:22.850
FAYE MOSS: And you also made up rhymes about your fights.

24
00:02:22.851 --> 00:02:30.663
MUHAMMAD ALI: I wrote poetry . My poetry got printed and quoted more than those poets the critics liked so

25
00:02:30.664 --> 00:02:41.542
much. I'll recite one for you: "Float like a butterfly, sting like a bee. The hands can't hit what the eyes can't

26
00:02:41.543 --> 00:02:45.270
see." FAYE MOSS: That sort of sums up your boxing style, doesn't

27
00:02:45.271 --> 00:02:50.021
it? MUHAMMAD ALI: I used to dance around a lot and let my opponent get tired chasing me

28
00:02:50.022 --> 00:02:55.843
around. I trained hard so I could last longer. When the other guy was tired, I'd finish him

29
00:02:55.844 --> 00:03:00.112
off. At the 1960 Summer Olympics in Rome, I got a gold

30
00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:08.908
medal. Four years later, when I was 22, I won the title of World Heavyweight Champion from Sonny Liston, the big, ugly

31
00:03:08.909 --> 00:03:12.565
bear. FAYE MOSS: Around that time you changed your

32
00:03:12.566 --> 00:03:19.261
name. Before then you were known as Cassius Marcellus Clay, Jr. Why did you change your

33
00:03:19.262 --> 00:03:23.077
name? MUHAMMAD ALI: In the early 1960s I became interested in

34
00:03:23.078 --> 00:03:29.473
Islam. Many black Muslims were involved in civil rights causes and taking pride in our black

35
00:03:29.474 --> 00:03:37.734
heritage. Cassius Clay was a name that came from a white slaveholder, so I changed my name to show pride in my black

36
00:03:37.735 --> 00:03:43.167
heritage. FAYE MOSS: Things were going pretty well for you until you got drafted for the Vietnam

37
00:03:43.168 --> 00:03:50.447
War. When you refused to go into the army, you were arrested. Why did you do

38
00:03:50.448 --> 00:03:54.599
that? MUHAMMAD ALI: My religious faith prevented me from going to war and killing

39
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.632
people. My conscience wouldn't let me go shoot some poor, hungry

40
00:03:58.633 --> 00:04:03.860
people. They never did anything to me. They were just little babies and women and

41
00:04:03.861 --> 00:04:10.208
children. How could I shoot those poor people? No, I couldn't do that. I said, "just take me to

42
00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:16.115
jail." I was sentenced to five years in jail, and that was okay with me because I believed in what I was

43
00:04:16.116 --> 00:04:19.024
doing. Finally my case made it to the Supreme

44
00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:26.081
Court, and the justices agreed that my faith prevented me from going to war, so they reversed my

45
00:04:26.082 --> 00:04:29.433
sentence. FAYE MOSS: But your heavyweight title had been taken

46
00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:36.915
away. And you weren't allowed to fight for three years while your court case was being heard! That must have been

47
00:04:36.916 --> 00:04:42.061
hard. MUHAMMAD ALI: No, not really. I was stopped from fighting, but I wasn't stopped from

48
00:04:42.062 --> 00:04:46.238
talking. I spent those years making speeches and visiting

49
00:04:46.239 --> 00:04:53.325
colleges. I spoke about my opposition to war. I spoke about Islam. I made a lot of

50
00:04:53.326 --> 00:05:00.206
friends. Eventually I was allowed to box again, and I got my title back. I was still the

51
00:05:00.207 --> 00:05:11.187
greatest. FAYE MOSS: You officially retired from boxing in 1981 with a record of 56 wins, 5 losses, and 37

52
00:05:11.188 --> 00:05:16.236
knockouts. You were not only great in the ring, you were also great out of

53
00:05:16.237 --> 00:05:21.446
it. In your later years you were well known for your humanitarian

54
00:05:21.447 --> 00:05:27.534
work. That's a long way from bashing people in the head in a boxing

55
00:05:27.535 --> 00:05:31.592
ring! MUHAMMAD ALI: Charity and good deeds were important to me as part of my

56
00:05:31.593 --> 00:05:36.115
faith. I always said that being the world heavyweight champion felt very small

57
00:05:36.116 --> 00:05:41.826
when I saw how millions of my poor black brothers and sisters were struggling in America.

58
00:05:41.827 --> 00:05:49.413
I visited historically black colleges and universities, and I donated money for scholarships for black students.

59
00:05:49.414 --> 00:05:57.343
Not only that, I traveled around the world to raise awareness on a variety of issues and see what good I could do.

60
00:05:57.344 --> 00:06:01.852
I went to places like Ghana, Iraq, and North Korea.

61
00:06:01.853 --> 00:06:06.665
One time I even stopped a man from jumping off a building in Los Angeles.

62
00:06:06.666 --> 00:06:12.452
FAYE MOSS: You also worked to raise awareness for Parkinson's disease, which affects the nervous system.

63
00:06:12.453 --> 00:06:16.600
More than ten million people around the world suffer from it.

64
00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:23.148
MUHAMMAD ALI: Yes, in the 1980s I was diagnosed with the disease, so it was a very personal issue.

65
00:06:23.149 --> 00:06:27.367
But I stayed healthy for a long time even after my diagnosis.

66
00:06:27.368 --> 00:06:30.914
FAYE MOSS: And you continued to do good work too.

67
00:06:30.915 --> 00:06:37.100
Thank you so much for talking with us today. We'll let you get back to history now.

68
00:06:37.101 --> 00:06:42.387
MUHAMMAD ALI: Thank you for having me, Faye!

69
00:06:42.388 --> 00:06:51.821
FAYE MOSS: In 1996 Ali lit the Olympic flame, and in 2012 he was chosen as a bearer for the Olympic flag.

70
00:06:51.822 --> 00:06:58.219
During his lifetime Ali received awards and recognition for his humanitarian work.

71
00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:02.404
In 2016 he died of Parkinson's disease.

72
00:07:02.405 --> 00:07:10.266
World leaders and dignitaries from many countries praised him for his effort for peace and civil rights.

73
00:07:10.267 --> 00:07:19.893
Ali once said he wanted to be remembered "as a man who treated everyone right and helped as many of his people as he could."

74
00:07:19.894 --> 00:07:23.037
I think he accomplished that goal.

75
00:07:23.038 --> 00:07:32.868
I'll see you again next time with another one of history's greatest stars!

